    "finish_elapsed_s": 11.8829839330283,
    "text": "A refrigerator keeps food cold by moving heat from inside to outside, like carrying warmth away in a bucket. A special liquid called refrigerant flows through pipes. First, it evaporates inside the fridge, soaking up heat from your food and air. That turns it into a gas. Then a compressor squeezes the gas, making it hot. Next, the hot gas flows through coils behind or under the fridge, where it releases heat into your kitchen and turns back into a liquid. Finally, it cycles back inside to absorb more heat. Because heat keeps getting removed, the inside stays cold—usually around 3°C (37°F)—so bacteria grow slowly and food lasts longer."
  }
